# Runs of 30 identical tokens, spread over at least 4 lines, shared by two or
# more crates.
#
# The floors are measured rather than chosen. At a 20-token window the
# workspace has 606 matches; at 45 it has one. Without the line spread the
# widest finding is thirteen files sharing the `impl Display` preamble, which
# is thirty tokens of Rust and no decision at all — a declaration packs its
# tokens into two lines, copied reasoning spreads them out.
#
# What this rule does NOT catch, said out loud so nobody reads a green gate as
# a clean tree:
#
#   - Twins that have already drifted. Five duration renderers were found by
#     hand precisely because they had diverged, and exact tokens cannot see
#     that. This rule catches copies before they rot, not after.
#   - Short law. `replace('-', "_")` inlined four times is six tokens, far
#     below any window that would not drown in false positives.
#   - Dense copies. The `is_ascii_alphanumeric() || c == '_' || …` character
#     class below is caught between cook-cli and cook-lang, where it is written
#     across several lines. A THIRD copy of the same predicate exists at
#     cook-contracts/src/probe_key/mod.rs:74 and cook-plan/src/registers.rs:627
#     as a one-liner, and this rule cannot see it. It is real work either way.
#
# Keyed on the files involved, not on the matching tokens, so editing inside a
# known clone does not churn the baseline into a rewrite nobody reviews.

{"key": "crates/cook-cache/src/probe.rs == crates/cook-contracts/src/context/mod.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-cache/src/probe.rs:131", "crates/cook-contracts/src/context/mod.rs:161"], "why": "Debt, and the sharpest one here: the SHA-256 digest-to-[u8;32] step written twice, one copy of it inside the crate that owns the hashing law. Hashing decides cache identity, and this cache crosses machines, so two implementations of one digest is the highest-stakes duplication in the workspace."}
{"key": "crates/cook-cli/src/main.rs == crates/cook-lang/src/lexer.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/main.rs:392", "crates/cook-lang/src/lexer.rs:128"], "why": "Debt: the character class that decides what may appear in a preset name, implemented by the lexer that parses one and again by the CLI that validates one. Grammar is admissible law in cook-contracts by the bar's own list, and the two ends disagreeing means a preset the CLI accepts and the parser refuses. A third copy exists as a one-liner this rule is too coarse to see."}
{"key": "crates/cook-cli/src/pipeline.rs == crates/cook-engine/src/run.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-cli/src/pipeline.rs:676", "crates/cook-engine/src/run.rs:349"], "why": "Debt: the empty RunResult, constructed field by field in the crate that defines it and again in the crate that calls it. A new field gets a value in one place and a default in the other, and nothing says which is right. The type should offer the empty value it wants callers to use."}
{"key": "crates/cook-engine/src/executor.rs == crates/cook-shell/src/lib.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-engine/src/executor.rs:141", "crates/cook-shell/src/lib.rs:141"], "why": "Debt: joining captured chunks of one stream into text, in the crate that captures them and in the crate that consumes them. cook-shell already owns the chunk type; the fold over it belongs there too, or with the chunk in cook-contracts."}
{"key": "crates/cook-engine/src/lib.rs == crates/cook-progress/src/event.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-engine/src/lib.rs:154", "crates/cook-progress/src/event.rs:82"], "why": "Debt: NodeKind declared twice, once in the engine that produces the events and once in the progress crate that renders them. Two enums for one vocabulary is the shape the registration-summary family was consolidated to remove; this one was missed because the names match and nothing compares them."}
{"key": "crates/cook-lua-stdlib/src/fs_api.rs == crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-lua-stdlib/src/fs_api.rs:173", "crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs:130"], "why": "Debt, mild: filling a Lua table from a path list, including the 1-based index convention Lua requires. Small, but it is a marshalling rule with two authors, and cook-lua-stdlib is the declared home for Lua-touching law."}
{"key": "crates/cook-luaotp/src/pool.rs == crates/cook-register/src/capture.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-luaotp/src/pool.rs:759", "crates/cook-register/src/capture.rs:603"], "why": "Debt: the member_to_string door implemented in both VMs, matching the duplicate-literal finding for its name and its diagnostic. The name, the message and now the body are all duplicated, which is a whole door with two implementations rather than one shared definition."}
{"key": "crates/cook-luaotp/src/pool.rs == crates/cook-register/src/module_loader.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-luaotp/src/pool.rs:1007", "crates/cook-register/src/module_loader.rs:292"], "why": "Debt, and it is already divergent: both build the cache.scope table the same way, but one installs a setter that writes and the other installs one that raises a deprecation error. The scaffolding agrees and the behaviour does not, which is exactly the state a shared definition would have made visible."}
{"key": "crates/cook-plan/src/registers.rs == crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs", "sites": ["crates/cook-plan/src/registers.rs:538", "crates/cook-register/src/dep_output_api.rs:248"], "why": "Debt: resolving an alias-qualified name against an importee prefix, implemented in the planner and again in the register-phase API. This is import-namespace law, it decides which recipe a name refers to across a workspace, and a disagreement between the two would route a dependency to the wrong recipe silently."}
